# `MEMBERSHIP-KEY-ACCEPTANCE`: `in=` is WITHDRAWN from `flowchart`. `SCENE-KEYWORD-MEMBERSHIP` stopped this
# genre declaring `group`, and a `group` id was `in=`'s ONLY value domain, so
# every value of the key was a dead end — `in=g` answered
# `unknown group "g"` and NO spelling of `g` succeeded, because no flowchart
# document can declare one. An unknown-option error that names the reason
# beats a dangling reference no author can satisfy, and the message points at
# `class=`, which is what expresses membership here today.
#
# All four acceptors of `in=` in this genre are written, because a key
# stranded on one directive need not be stranded on another: `node` and the
# three role lines resolve `in=` against declared GROUP ids and nothing else.
# The other two acceptors in the LANGUAGE — `threshold` and `band`, whose
# domain `MARKER-TARGET-KINDS` widened to REGION ids — are not this genre's keywords since
# `SCENE-KEYWORD-MEMBERSHIP`, which is why the withdrawal is by KEY and not directive by directive.
#
# The last line is the check that made "stranded" measurable rather than
# assumed: a flowchart document CAN declare a region (`table` is a `GENRE-COMPOSITION` region
# opener, legal in every scene genre), and `MARKER-TARGET-KINDS`'s widening still never reached
# `node` — `node e "E" in=q` over a `table q` answered `unknown group "q"`,
# not a region binding. If a later ruling widens `node`'s `in=` to regions,
# this line is the fixture that must change.
#
# The header is `figdown 0.2` deliberately: the withdrawal is NOT gated by the
# language version, because `flowchart` is EXPERIMENTAL and outside the
# compatibility promise (`EDGE-GEOMETRY-CONSTRUCTS` precedent — no gate, no rewrite owed).
figdown 0.2 flowchart
node a "A" in=g
process b "B" in=g
decision c "C" in=g
terminator d "D" in=g
table q
| Head |
| ---- |
| 1    |

node e "E" in=q
